    "description": "Digital skills encompass finding and evaluating information, communicating, creating content, protecting accounts, and solving practical problems with technology. Assessing these capabilities in Iran requires skills evidence distinct from statistics about Internet use or connectivity.",
    "tags": ["Digital literacy", "Digital inclusion", "ICT skills", "Lifelong learning", "Education"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T17:43:46Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T17:43:46Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Digital Skills in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand digital literacy, how ICT skills are measured, and why Iranian Internet-use statistics cannot establish practical digital competence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "meaning-and-scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The capabilities behind everyday technology",
        "content": "Digital skills extend beyond learning to code. The International Telecommunication Union, the United Nations specialized agency for information and communication technologies, includes searching online, communicating, evaluating information, and using work-related platforms within its discussion of basic skills. More specialized capabilities include programming, data analysis, artificial intelligence, and cybersecurity. For a guide to Iran, this distinction prevents professional technology training from standing in for the much broader question of everyday digital participation. [s1]\n\nIt also changes how access should be understood. ITU identifies home connectivity, training opportunities, and financial circumstances as factors affecting skills development. These are useful questions for research into Iran, but the international framework alone does not establish which Iranian communities experience particular disadvantages or how large those disadvantages are. Local evidence is needed before attributing a measured gap to geography, income, or another characteristic. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measurement",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How skills are measured",
        "content": "ITU's explanation of Sustainable Development Goal indicator 4.4.1 organizes ICT skills into information and data literacy, communication and collaboration, digital content creation, safety, and problem solving. Rather than relying only on a person's general assessment of their ability, measurement asks about recently performed activities that imply relevant proficiency. This is an activity-based proxy, not a comprehensive practical examination of everything a person can do. [s2]\n\nA useful Iranian skills profile would therefore identify the particular activity, the population surveyed, the reference period, and the denominator behind each percentage. Results for all individuals and results for Internet users answer different questions. Likewise, reported performance of an activity should not automatically be described as expert competence. ITU explicitly warns that skills reporting is incomplete internationally, so missing observations cannot be interpreted as an absence of skills. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading Iranian evidence carefully",
        "content": "UNdata provides an Iran-specific series for the percentage of individuals using the Internet, sourced from ITU's World Telecommunication/ICT Indicators Database. Its explanatory notes describe collection through regulatory agencies, ministries, and statistical offices, followed by verification and harmonization. The displayed series also carries observation-specific footnotes, including estimates and age-coverage qualifications. Those notes matter when interpreting a long timeline. [s3]\n\nHowever, Internet use is a different indicator from digital skills. It cannot establish whether users can assess information, protect accounts, or complete unfamiliar tasks. This page therefore does not relabel connectivity observations as a skills trend. A publication-ready national assessment needs the underlying Iranian skills tables and their survey metadata, including comparable breakdowns and reporting periods. Until those are inspected, the defensible conclusion is methodological: distinguish participation online from the capabilities needed to participate effectively. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
